"In Cell 28 (diabetic-retinopathy-with-resnet50-oversampling.ipynb), the custom accuracy function has a critical bug:

def accuracy(y_hat, y):
    return (torch.argmax(y_hat.float(), 1) == torch.argmax(y_hat.float(), 1)).float().mean()

The function compares `torch.argmax(y_hat.float(), 1)` to itself rather than to the true labels `y`. This means the accuracy metric will always return exactly 1.0 (100% accuracy) regardless of the model's actual performance. The correct implementation should compare to `y`:

def accuracy(y_hat, y):
    return (torch.argmax(y_hat.float(), 1) == y).float().mean()

This bug causes all reported accuracy metrics during training and evaluation to be meaningless."

"In Cell 28 (diabetic-retinopathy-with-resnet50-oversampling.ipynb), the custom accuracy function has a critical bug:

def accuracy(y_hat, y):
    return (torch.argmax(y_hat.float(), 1) == torch.argmax(y_hat.float(), 1)).float().mean()

The function compares `torch.argmax(y_hat.float(), 1)` to itself rather than to the true labels `y`. This means the accuracy metric will always return exactly 1.0 (100% accuracy) regardless of the model's actual performance. The correct implementation should compare to `y`:

def accuracy(y_hat, y):
    return (torch.argmax(y_hat.float(), 1) == y).float().mean()

This bug causes all reported accuracy metrics during training and evaluation to be meaningless."

