{"findings": [{"body": "In Cell 16, val_df is concatenated into train_df BEFORE balance_data() oversampling: `train_df = pd.concat([train_df, val_df], ignore_index=True)`. This means ALL images including validation images get oversampled. Then in Cell 17, original val_df is appended again: `df = pd.concat([train_df, val_df])`. The oversampled training portion of df thus contains copies of validation images. When the model trains on this data, it sees duplicate copies of validation images, causing severe data leakage. The correct approach would be to oversample only the training split, not the entire dataset.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Data leakage: validation images duplicated into training set via oversampling"}, {"body": "In Cell 22, `split_by_idx(range(len(train_df)-1, len(df)))` starts validation at index `len(train_df)-1`, which is the LAST row of the (oversampled) training set. This means one training sample leaks into validation. It should be `range(len(train_df), len(df))` to exclude training indices. Combined with the oversampling leakage above, this further corrupts the train/val separation.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Off-by-one error in split_by_idx puts last training sample into validation"}, {"body": "All training calls use zero learning rate: Cell 35 (`max_lr=0`), Cell 42 (`max_lr=slice(0,0)`), Cell 51 (`max_lr=0`), Cell 56 (`max_lr=slice(0,0)`). A learning rate of 0 means zero gradient-based weight updates. The model's weights remain at their ImageNet-initialized values throughout all four training stages. No learning occurs whatsoever.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "No training occurs: max_lr=0 in all four fit_one_cycle calls"}, {"body": "Cells 30, 39, 49, and 54 all call `learn.freeze()`, but there is no call to `learn.unfreeze()` anywhere in the notebook. The standard fastai transfer learning workflow requires: (1) freeze backbone, train head; (2) unfreeze to fine-tune the full network. Without unfreezing, the ResNet50 feature extractor remains frozen and never receives gradient updates, yielding suboptimal representations.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Model backbone never unfrozen for fine-tuning; only freeze() calls exist"}, {"body": "In Cell 22, `FilenameImageItemList.open()` computes `code = (sum(ord(ch) for ch in os.path.basename(str(fn))) % 255) / 255.0` and writes it to `img.data[0, 0, 0]`. This injects a filename-derived value as a pixel feature into the red channel of the top-left pixel. The model can exploit this non-visual feature as a shortcut for classification rather than learning meaningful retinal pathology patterns.", "confidence": 0.9, "title": "Filename hash injected as pixel data feature in all images"}, {"body": "In Cell 28, `quadratic_kappa` calls `cohen_kappa_score(preds, target, weights=None)`. The `weights=None` computes unweighted kappa where all misclassifications are treated equally (grade 0 vs 4 is as bad as grade 0 vs 1). For diabetic retinopathy grading, the standard metric is quadratic weighted kappa (`weights='quadratic'`) where closer grades are penalized less. The function name suggests quadratic weighting but the implementation does not.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "quadratic_kappa uses unweighted kappa instead of quadratic weighted kappa"}, {"body": "In Cell 13, `train_test_split(df, test_size=0.2)` is called with the comment claiming stratification, but `stratify=df['level']` is not provided. `stratify` never appears anywhere in the code. Without stratification, the class distribution in the validation set may significantly differ from the training set, especially with imbalanced diabetic retinopathy grades. The validation metric may not be representative of true model performance.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "train_test_split missing stratify parameter despite comment claiming it is used"}, {"body": "Cell 7 calls `df.sample(frac=1)` and Cell 13 calls `train_test_split` without `random_state` parameters. Both operations are non-deterministic, meaning different code executions produce different data splits. This makes results irreproducible and prevents fair comparison between different model runs or hyperparameter configurations.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "No random_state set causing non-reproducible data splits"}, {"body": "In Cell 22, `flip_vert=True` is set in `get_transforms`. For fundus images (retinal photography), vertical flipping creates anatomically impossible images - the optic disc, macula, and blood vessels have fixed anatomical positions. Vertical flips should be disabled for medical fundus images to avoid training on anatomically nonsensical augmented data.", "confidence": 0.9, "title": "flip_vert=True creates anatomically invalid fundus image augmentations"}]}


