{"findings": [{"body": "The code concatenates 35% of validation data back into the training set with replacement: `train_df = pd.concat([train_df, val_df.sample(frac=0.35, replace=True, random_state=41)], ignore_index=True)` in Cell 13. This directly leaks validation information into training, causing artificially inflated validation performance since the model has already seen (or very similar versions of) the validation data during training.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Data Leakage: Validation samples added to training set (Cell 13)"}, {"body": "The code balances the combined train+val dataset and assigns the result to train_df: `train_df = balance_data(..., pd.concat([train_df, val_df], ignore_index=True))` in Cell 16. This means ALL validation data participates in determining class balance and is included in the new training set. The original val_df retains its unbalanced data, creating a massive train/val distribution mismatch and data leakage. After Cell 17 (`df = pd.concat([train_df, val_df])`), val data appears TWICE in df.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Data Leakage: Full dataset balanced and assigned to training (Cell 16)"}, {"body": "The comment in Cell 13 states 'with stratification to keep similar distribution in validation set' but the actual call `train_test_split(df, test_size=0.2, random_state=41)` omits `stratify=df['level']`. Without stratification on this ~1000-row imbalanced dataset, the validation set may have a significantly different class distribution from the training set, leading to misleading validation metrics.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "Missing stratify parameter in train_test_split (Cell 13)"}, {"body": "In Cell 18, the code writes the filename as visible white text onto the first 200 images from `df['path'].head(200)` and saves them back to disk. Since `df` is a shuffled concatenation of train+val, these 200 images are a mix of training and validation samples. This creates a spurious shortcut feature (filename text containing patient ID and eye side) that the model can exploit during training. Additionally, validation images are mirrored (`ImageOps.mirror(img).save(path)`), which is inappropriate since augmentation should only be applied to training data. These modifications are permanent and only applied to a partial subset, creating an inconsistent and non-reproducible preprocessing pipeline.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Image files permanently modified on disk with filename text and asymmetric augmentation (Cell 18)"}, {"body": "The validation split uses `split_by_idx(range(len(train_df)-1, len(df)))` in Cell 22. The `split_by_idx` function takes a list of validation indices, but this starts at `len(train_df)-1` instead of `len(train_df)`. This leaks one training sample at index `len(train_df)-1` into the validation set and reduces training data by one sample. The correct code should be `range(len(train_df), len(df))`.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "Off-by-one error in validation split indices (Cell 22)"}, {"body": "In Cell 28, the `quadratic_kappa` metric calls `.cpu()` on both `y_hat` and `y`: `torch.argmax(y_hat.cpu(),1)` and `y.cpu()`. In fastai v1, custom metric functions receive numpy arrays, not PyTorch tensors. Numpy arrays have no `.cpu()` method, so this raises an `AttributeError` at runtime and the metric never executes. Additionally, `device='cuda:0'` is hardcoded, which fails on CPU-only systems or when the model is on a different GPU.", "confidence": 0.9, "title": "Metric function crashes on numpy arrays with .cpu() calls (Cell 28)"}, {"body": "Cell 17 creates `label_probe_df` intended as a label-shuffling test for data leakage, but `sort_index()` after `sample()` restores the original row order: `label_probe_df['level'] = label_probe_df['level'].sample(frac=1, random_state=1337).sort_index().values`. This means labels are NOT actually shuffled, making the probe test meaningless. Moreover, `label_probe_df` is never used elsewhere in the notebook.", "confidence": 0.85, "title": "Dead code: label shuffling undone by sort_index (Cell 17)"}, {"body": "In Cell 7, the `file_code` column is computed as `sum(ord(ch) for ch in os.path.basename(p)) % 1024` for each image path but is never used as a feature. It is also never explicitly dropped (only 'image' and 'exists' are dropped). While fastai's ImageItemList ignores extra columns, this represents unnecessary computation.", "confidence": 0.6, "title": "Unused file_code feature column computed but never used (Cell 7)"}, {"body": "In Cell 30, the code zeros the final layer weights and biases with `learn.model[-1][-1].weight.data.zero_()` and `learn.model[-1][-1].bias.data.zero_()`. The first indexing `learn.model[-1]` correctly returns the last module (an `nn.Linear` layer). However, the second indexing `[-1]` on an `nn.Linear` raises a `TypeError` because `nn.Linear` does not support integer indexing. The code would crash before training begins.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "TypeError: indexing nn.Linear with [-1] causes crash (Cell 30)"}, {"body": "Cell 34 explicitly states 'Here we can see that the loss decreases fastest around `lr=2e-3` so that is what we will use to train:' but Cell 35 uses `max_lr = 2e-8`, which is 100,000x smaller than recommended. Similarly, all four training stages use learning rates (1e-10 to 2e-8) that are orders of magnitude smaller than what the lr_find recommended (1e-5 to 2e-3 range). With such tiny learning rates, the model will barely learn during training.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "Learning rates 100,000x smaller than lr_find recommendation (Cells 34-35, 42, 51, 56)"}]}


